      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description expressly identifies both competing forum provisions and asks which controls. Comparing that stated relationship with d02 requires no additional factual verification or interpretive steps."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 6,
      "estimated_minutes": 10,
      "reason": "The reviewer must separate the reservation's effect on initial forum choice from the description's alternative characterization as merely consent to personal jurisdiction. That requires comparing d04's initial-choice-versus-later-transfer distinction with the neighboring d03 issue and deciding what problem the combined description actually names."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description identifies the Service of Suit undertaking, the New York forum requirement, and the supersession question directly. The reference to Condition 5.10 is readily traceable to the opinion's numbering, so identifying the two provisions adds little work."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 5,
      "estimated_minutes": 6,
      "reason": "The reviewer must test the assertion that 'other terms ... remain unchanged' preserves the New York clause against the court's use of that sentence as evidence that the preceding endorsement terms changed the policy. The additional judgment is whether the description's saving-clause-versus-undertaking framing names the same coherence question despite presenting a different mechanism."
    }
